/// Hashes for security, HMAC and the system's random bytes (design/v5/spec/10 Q4): SHA-256 and
/// SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4), SHA-1 (FIPS 180-4) and MD5 (RFC 1321) — the last two for what an old
/// format asks, never where an attacker chooses the input —, HMAC over any of them (RFC 2104),
/// random bytes from the system, and a comparison of bytes that takes as long whatever it finds.
/// The doors (U2): Ed25519, X25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, Argon2. All in Lyric; the system's bytes
/// come through the runtime's `lyr_random_u64`, which `std.hash` keys its hasher from too.

extern "C" fn lyr_random_u64(): uint64;

/// A hash fed in pieces (10 Q4): `update` as often as there are pieces, `finish` for the hash of
/// all of them. `finish` leaves the digest as it was, so it may be fed further and finished again.
/// The decision writes `finish(): uint8[N]`; an interface cannot name a length that is each
/// implementer's own — 5.0 has no generic constant (03 T13) —, so it answers a `uint8[]` of `size`
/// bytes, and the one-liner `sha256` an `uint8[32]`. `blockSize` and `new` are what HMAC needs of
/// a digest it is generic over.
pub interface Digest {
    /// The length of the hash, in bytes.
    static let size: int;
    /// The length of the blocks it hashes, in bytes: what HMAC pads its key to.
    static let blockSize: int;

    static fn new(): Self;
    mut fn update(bytes: Slice<uint8>): void;
    fn finish(): uint8[];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The constants (FIPS 180-4 §4.2.2, §4.2.3, §5.3.3, §5.3.5; RFC 1321 §3.4), computed from their
// definitions — the fractional parts of the square and cube roots of the first primes, |sin(i)| —
// rather than copied, and checked against the standards' printed values.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

